{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "22",
    "case_summary": "A 21-year-old patient from Sudan, socially identified as female, was referred for evaluation due to delayed puberty. The patient was assigned female at birth by a midwife and raised accordingly. The patient identifies as female and was hesitant to discuss sexual attraction, possibly due to cultural reasons. Physical examination revealed the patient was dressed in female attire, had no facial hair, displayed muscular build, broad shoulders, wide nipple spacing, and lacked breast development. The patient measured approximately 175 cm in height and had a female voice. Examination of the external genitalia showed Type IV FGM, with no pubic hair or increased pigmentation in the genital area. A rectal examination suggested the presence of a prostate. Hormonal analysis showed a serum prolactin level of 215 Mu/L, FSH of 0.5 Mu/L, and LH of 1.1 Mu/L (normal ranges: serum prolactin: 60–370 Mu/L, serum FSH: 1–8 Mu/L, serum LH: 2–10 Mu/L).\n\nCytogenetic analysis showed a normal male karyotype, 46, XY. Ultrasound examination confirmed the presence of a 15 × 15 mm diameter soft tissue mass in the position of the male prostate.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
    "root": {
      "label": "Chronic Progressive Disorder of Sex Development with Normal Male Karyotype",
      "time_course": "chronic",
      "severity": "unspecified",
      "confidence": 0.8,
      "supporting_facts": [
